\title{A Note on Hash Functions in Bitcoin and Ethereum}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    [Hash Functions in Bitcoin and Ethereum](dhsorens-004D) is about what each chain actually hashes
    with, and about the Ethereum Foundation abandoning Poseidon for L1 on 13 August 2026. The reason
    is the interesting part: Poseidon did not break. Proof systems over binary fields made
    conventional hashes cheap enough to prove that decades of public cryptanalysis became the
    deciding factor instead.
}

\p{
    The sections:
    \ul{
        \li{[SHA-2 and the Bitcoin design](dhsorens-004E) — double SHA-256 for proof of work and
        transaction identifiers, RIPEMD-160 for legacy addresses, tagged hashes for Taproot.}
        \li{[Keccak and Ethereum](dhsorens-004F) — why \code{keccak256} is not SHA-3, everywhere it
        names state, and the SHA-256 half of the protocol on the consensus layer.}
        \li{[The BLAKE family](dhsorens-004G) — 2s against 2b, what BLAKE3's tree mode buys, and
        which variant is actually a candidate.}
        \li{[Poseidon, and why Ethereum walked away](dhsorens-004H) — the HADES construction, the
        cryptanalysis initiative, and the Binius and Flock results that reversed the decision.}
    }
}
